hi my name is Betsy Bradley and I'm the  faculty director of the global health  Leadership Institute at Yale today I'm  joined by David Berg who is a clinical  professor in psychiatry at the Yale  School of Medicine and an organizational  psychologist and today we're here to  talk about leadership you know everybody  and David you know everybody is studying  leadership everybody talks about it  so it's one of the fundamental things if  you've got a new job you need to learn  how to be a leader and have those  leadership skills so I wanted to start  us with what do you think are the most  common misunderstandings about  leadership well I'd say there's probably  three so the first one is that  leadership is a trait of a person that  one is either born with or that one has  as a characteristic of self from growing  up etc and I I don't think it's actually  a trait I don't think it's something  that in here is in a person I think as  we'll talk about I'm sure I think it's a  function in an organization or a social  setting  so one misunderstanding is that people  have it or can be given it it's a trait  the second thing I would say is that  only one person does it so when we talk  about leadership we tend to use words  like leaders that suggest that  leadership is done by one person I think  that's the second thing I don't I don't  believe it's ever done in a social  system by one person and the third thing  I would say is that there's only one way  to do it you there's just too much  diversity of people in leadership roles  in the way in which people in those  roles are successful in different kinds  of settings and different kinds of  organizations to believe that there's  only one right way to do leadership and  yet we tend to perpetuate the belief by  and theories that there's one way to do  it right even contingency theories will  say under these circumstances do it one  particular way I think it's over  emphasized the one-wayness do you mind  just talking a little bit about what do  you mean when you say contingency theory  so there are lots of different phases in  the evolution of leadership theory and  one of those was called contingency  theory which said that the behavior  action of someone in a leadership role  depends on the circumstances the  circumstances of the task or the job  being done the circumstances of the  people being worked with those kinds of  things so I want to ask you about  business schools and management schools  because I think they grew out of  thinking there was one way you just need  to take these courses and you will be a  leader you'll be successful and I'm  wondering that still seems to be around  and what do you think of that and how do  you think we ought to be thinking about  developing the skill of somebody to lead  well so I think it's right I think I  think most education in the area of  leadership is based on the assumption  that leadership is something in a person  that you can develop or teach or train  and while I don't think that's a hundred  percent wrong I think what it misses is  the fact that leadership I believe is  fundamentally a relational concept that  is it comes into being when there's a  relationship without a relationship  there is no leadership so you as faculty  director of things when you're at home  sitting in your dining room by yourself  you are not a leader you can't be and if  you're thinking of yourself as a leader  you're thinking of yourself in one or  more different relational context  because that's what creates leadership  it is a function for a social system the  smallest social system being two people  but usually it's about a faculty group  or about an organization and the  leadership function comes into being  when that when one or more different  kinds of  ships exist so I would say we're going  to be more successful at teaching people  in various professional schools business  schools being one about leadership to  the extent that we begin to focus as  much on the kinds of relationship work  that needs to be done to effectively  move an organization towards its task or  its goal than just focusing on  individual skills and trade so I have  heard you say leadership is a role and  then you just said it's really a  relational concept can you sort of  describe how those two sort of  statements of what leadership is fit  together so I'll give you an example  when I sometimes work with groups of  executives or students and I'm being  slightly polemical and I say so you've  heard the phrase lead follow or get out  of the way yeah and and I say so that  suggests to you that you either lead or  you follow or in the worst case you make  yourself scarce and I say okay don't be  don't be shy or self-deprecating I need  you to choose which one of those you are  whether you're a leader or a follower  and everybody in the room gets very  nervous and they get sort of squeamish  and they don't particularly door a  follower and then I say to them okay I  won't make you choose let's say I asked  you how many people in this room are in  leadership roles and everybody's hand  goes up and then I say how many of you  are in followership roles and  everybody's hand goes up and then we  begin to see that the leadership and  followership are roles not traits or  characteristic of a person because the  vast majority of people I would say  actually a hundred percent of people who  work in organizations are in both roles  so role again means a set of activities  expected of somebody who inhabits a  certain place in an organization I would  say leadership is a role you have in  relationship to some people and in  to other people you are in a  followership role in relationship to  those people of the hierarchy  you
 

